    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Recurrent transient episodes of collapse: six days ago a witnessed collapse with stiffening and jerking of both arms for about a minute, a bitten tongue, and about twenty minutes of confusion, after a rising strange feeling and dizziness; two briefer spells of staring and unresponsiveness in the past eighteen months; cause not yet known (seizure, convulsive syncope, or transient ischemic attack)</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R56.9</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented accounts of falling attacks and fainting fits; it resolves when the cause is found.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Hypertension, stage 2: 152/94 and 148/92 mmHg today; told of high blood pressure about three years ago and never treated</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I10</td><td>S — Synthetic vascular risk factor; no blood pressure from his lifetime is known.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Hyperlipidemia: LDL 158 mg/dL, triglycerides 170 mg/dL, HDL 44 mg/dL</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E78.5</td><td>S — Synthetic vascular risk factor; no lipid value from his lifetime is known.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Focal epilepsy with a structural vascular cause: focal impaired-awareness seizures with automatisms, one evolving to a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure, arising from a chronic left temporal cortical infarct, with left temporal sharp waves on EEG</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G40.209</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis anchored to the documented falling attacks; the ILAE 2017 seizure types and a structural cause are chosen for teaching and make no claim about what he had.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Chronic left posterior temporal cortical infarct, not recognized before the seizures, with moderate small-vessel white matter disease and a non-stenotic left carotid plaque; no residual deficit</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I69.398</td><td>S — Synthetic modern finding anchored to the retrospective cerebrovascular hypothesis; it makes no claim about his real brain.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection with low-grade fever and poor sleep, three weeks after starting levetiracetam; rapid COVID-19 and influenza tests negative; resolved</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent illness, chosen to teach cold-remedy choices with hypertension, aspirin, and an antiseizure medicine, and sleep loss as a seizure trigger; it echoes no documented illness.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

